gm-method (a NEW render change is blamed for a GL error the moment getError returns non-zero after it -- but getError is a STICKY latch that accumulates from ANY draw that frame, so a single read cannot attribute the error). Prove ownership with a LIVE A/B TOGGLE, not reasoning: gate the new pass behind a window.__featureOff flag, then measure getError (drained-then-N-frames) with the pass ON, OFF, and ON again. If the error is IDENTICAL across all three, it is PRE-EXISTING (here: a 1282 'Insufficient buffer size' that turned out to be a sibling cluster-mesh draw + a 404'd progressive asset in a stale dev-server instance, not the new water-depth pass). A per-frame COUNTER incremented inside the new pass, read alongside, double-confirms the toggle actually engages (freezes when OFF, climbs when ON) so an 'identical error' reading can't be dismissed as 'the toggle didn't take'. This A/B-with-counter converts 'did my change break GL?' from a guess into a one-run witness. Corollary: a stale long-lived dev server serving an old node_modules can 404 a progressive/baked asset that then falls back to an error-emitting path -- verify the asset 200s before attributing its downstream GL error to your edit.